/** \page checkpointing Exact ODE checkpointing and replay

MFEM's checkpointing API reconstructs exact primal states without requiring a
complete forward trajectory to remain in memory. The initial implementation is
synchronous and supports fixed-step ForwardEulerSolver through an opt-in
ForwardEulerCheckpointAdapter.

\section checkpointing_architecture Architecture

\code{.unparsed}
TimeDependentOperator / ODESolver
                 |
                 v
      ODESolverCheckpointAdapter
                 |
                 v
       CheckpointController
          |             |
          v             v
 StoreEverything    exact replay
                          |
                          v
              ExactCheckpointWindow
                          |
                    +-----+-----+
                    |           |
                 memory       files
\endcode

The controller and adapters borrow their dependencies. The application owns
the ODESolver, TimeDependentOperator, adapter, propagator, storage backend, and
moving window, and must keep them alive for the complete controller operation.
The classes are not internally synchronized.

The solution Vector is not assumed to be a complete restart. CheckpointState
stores the Vector, an explicit TimePoint, the continuation step size, and opaque
solver restart bytes. Forward Euler has no persistent stage history, so its
restart payload is empty and Restore reinitializes the solver. Future adapters
for adaptive or multistep solvers must capture their complete controller and
history state in the restart payload.

\section checkpointing_persistence Persistence

Vector values are read through Vector::HostRead(), so checkpoint capture may
synchronize device data to host. Restore writes through Vector::HostWrite().
The MFEM-facing scalar type is real_t; persistent time, step-size, and Vector
values use IEEE binary64. Conversion from single-precision real_t to binary64
and back preserves every original value exactly.

The version 1 format has a 64-byte, canonical little-endian header:

\code{.unparsed}
offset  bytes  field
  0      8    magic 0x4d46454d43503100
  8      4    format version (1)
 12      1    byte-order marker (1 = little-endian)
 13      1    scalar width (8 = IEEE binary64)
 14      2    reserved, zero
 16      8    logical CheckpointId
 24      8    StepId bit representation
 32      8    physical time
 40      8    continuation dt
 48      8    Vector length
 56      8    restart payload length
 64     ...   Vector entries followed by opaque restart bytes
\endcode

Decoding rejects unsupported versions, malformed lengths, ID mismatches,
truncation, and trailing bytes. Invalid bytes raise InvalidCheckpointFormat;
ordinary filesystem failures raise CheckpointStorageError.

FileCheckpointStorage uses one deterministic file per logical checkpoint. An
overwrite is written and closed in a same-directory temporary file and then
renamed. A failed overwrite leaves the prior canonical file unchanged. Stale
temporary files are ignored, and no fsync or crash-durability guarantee is
made.

\section checkpointing_replay Exact replay

CheckpointController::Restore() restores exactly the requested logical
CheckpointId. CheckpointController::RestoreStep() first uses an exact target if
available; otherwise it selects the nearest preceding exact state in the
transient moving window or persistent storage and advances with the normal
ODESolver::Step() implementation. Failed restore or replay does not commit a
partial active state or inconsistent moving-window entry.

StoreEverythingSchedule provides the initial deterministic forward schedule.
It stores the initial state and every accepted forward state. More advanced
checkpoint schedules and reverse-time orchestration are intentionally outside
this initial exact-replay interface.

\section checkpointing_parallel Rank-local parallel compatibility

The checkpoint core contains no MPI calls. A parallel application may create a
separate storage root on each rank while using the same deterministic schedule
and logical CheckpointId values:

\code{.unparsed}
             common logical schedule
                       |
              +--------+--------+
              |        |        |
             r0       r1       r2
              |        |        |
      rank_000000  rank_000001  rank_000002
              local FileCheckpointStorage
\endcode

For logical checkpoint i on P ranks, the distributed application checkpoint is
the collection C_i = { C_i^(0), ..., C_i^(P-1) }. Each payload contains that
rank's local Vector portion. The application must create unique rank-local
directories and coordinate errors around collective operators.

This compatibility model does not provide collective commit, failure
propagation, cross-rank rollback, MPI-IO, a shared-file format, or
fault-tolerant recovery.

\section checkpointing_example Example

Example 42 compares ordinary fixed-step Forward Euler integration with a
trajectory replayed from its initial exact checkpoint. It reports
`terminal replay error: 0` for the default deterministic run.

The following concepts correspond to the standalone checkpoint library:

\code{.unparsed}
Standalone                       MFEM
StepId / CheckpointId            StepId / CheckpointId
Snapshot                         Snapshot
MemoryStorage / FileStorage      MemoryCheckpointStorage / FileCheckpointStorage
SnapshotWindow<Vector>           ExactCheckpointWindow
StoreEverythingSchedule          StoreEverythingSchedule
IntegratorCheckpointAdapter      ODESolverCheckpointAdapter
ForwardPropagator<Vector>        ODECheckpointPropagator
CheckpointExecutionController    CheckpointController
\endcode
*/
